imitedRetainedMetabolites)\n```\nYou are subtracting `0.0` from the organ\u0027s current contents. This makes the simulator think that **100% of the contents** are ready to be exported. Since your Stomach-to-Intestine edge is set to `100.0%` for its metabolites, it empties the source organ immediately every step.\n\n**The Fix:**\nPass the organ\u0027s current metabolites into the rate limiter so the ODE has a starting mass to work with:\n```kotlin\nval rateLimitedRetainedMetabolites \u003d it.processSubstrates(currentNode.organ.metabolitesMap, t0)\n```\n\n### 3. \"ReadOnly\" Zeroing (Check your Organ setup)\nIn your `Organ.kt` base class, you have this logic:\n\n```kotlin\nreadOnlyMetabolites.forEach { type -\u003e\n    if (metabolitesMap.contains(type)) metabolitesMap.updateQuantities(\n        Metabolite(type, 0.0) // \u003c--- Explicitly sets value to 0.0\n    )\n}\n```\nIf you accidentally added `MetaboliteType.INSULIN` to the `readOnlyMetabolites` set when defining the `Stomach` (similar to how you did for the `Pancreas`), the engine will reset Insulin to `0.0` at the end of every single time step, regardless of what was transported in.\n\n### Summary of what to check:\n1.  **Check the Graph:** If you want Insulin in the stomach, add a `DirectionalOrganEdge` from `Blood` to `Stomach` in `BodyTransportGraph.kt` and include `QuantifiedMetabolite(MetaboliteType.INSULIN, ...)` in the list.\n2.  **Fix `Body.kt`:** Ensure your `rateLimiter` processes `currentNode.organ.metabolitesMap` instead of an empty map.\n3.  **Check Scientific Notation:** In `formatWeight`, if the value is extremely small (e.g., $10^{-10}$), it might be displaying as scientific notation which can look like `0.0` at a glance, though your formatter handles this. If it says exactly `0.00 mmol`, it has been fully emptied or never arrived.",
