      "text": "Now, check this document for any grammatical mistakes. update things like the alpha character to render well, as well as scientific notation. Some names in tables use all caps and underscores -- change these to be natural language. Also update all of the citation keys to match the ones you just updated. DO NOT CHANGE CONTENT\n\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[table]{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{array}    % Required for better text alignment\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx} % Required for inserting images\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\setlength{\\parindent}{0pt}    % no paragraph indentation\n\\setlength{\\parskip}{1em}      % add vertical space between paragraphs\n\\usepackage[a4paper, margin\u003d1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{nicematrix}\n\\usepackage[table]{xcolor}\n\\newcommand{\\var}[1]{\\texttt{\\seqsplit{#1}}} \n\\usepackage{seqsplit} \n\\usepackage{xltabular}\n\\usepackage{threeparttable}\n\n\\title{Discretized, First-Order-Kinetics Simulation of the Catabolism Steps of Major Macronutrients of a Peanut in the Human Body}\n\\author{Jon Sharp \\\\\n    jon.sharp@ucdconnect.ie \\\\\n    UCD Student Number: 25278680\n    }\n\\date{December 22, 2025}\n\n\\usepackage[style\u003dauthoryear,backend\u003dbiber]{biblatex}\n\n\\addbibresource{references.bib}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle \n\n\\section{Introduction}\nThis paper presents a software simulation prototype of the catabolism of key peanut macronutrients within the human body. The simulation begins with chosen quantities of starch, proteins, and fats in the mouth. It concludes in the mitochondria, with final outputs of produced ATP or energy equivalents from the Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle and Electron Transport Chain (ETC). The simulation does not include major anabolic steps which influence the fate of peanut nutrients.\n\nA video demo of the simulation is accessible at the following link:\nhttps://drive.google.com/file/d/1iAvv22Su3pxp-vrJf5WH93K_mMKOf0L_/view?usp\u003dsharing\n\nTo construct this simulation, successful enzymatic reaction rate models have been selected from the literature. Individual catabolic steps are intended to appear realistic. However, the project prioritized learning and qualitative demonstration over quantitative accuracy. As such, as discussed below, strong assumptions made for the simulation model fail to capture true complexities of metabolism.\n\nFurther sections address simulation construction, models used in each organ and metabolic process, assumptions, and limitations. Most theory which influenced simulation design and discussion came from graduate course lecture content \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. This content has been explicitly cited below only in instances where specific numerical values were used in calculations, but was the basis for the majority of content design.\n\n\\section{Simulation Construction}\n\\subsection{Micronutrient Inputs}\n\\label{sec:micronutrient-inputs}\nTo represent a peanut beginning ingestion in the mouth, quantities of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins corresponding to 50 grams of whole peanuts were selected. For simplicity, the model only considered the most prominent of each of these macronutrients in peanuts -- starch, oleic acid (mono-unsaturated) \\autocite{settaluri_peanuts_2012}, and $\\alpha$-arachin \\autocite{cui_peanut_2023}, respectively. All triglycerides were assumed to be triolein.\n\nSee \\nameref{sec:physical-constants} for a complete list of sourced molar masses for these nutrients, and other relevant physical constants used in the model.\n\n\\subsection{Organ Pipeline Model}\n\\autoref{tab:organ-metabolizers} shows a list of all considered organs/organelles, alongside each metabolic process within that organ, and the processed substrates.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]                                                              \n  \\centering   \n  \\begin{threeparttable}\n  \\caption{Organs, Metabolic Agents, and Metabolites in the Peanut Metabolism Simulator}\n  \\label{tab:organ-metabolizers}                                                \n  \\small % Recommended to help fit 4 columns comfortably\n  \\begin{tabularx}{\\textwidth}{|l|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|}                                          \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  \\textbf{Organ} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolic Agent} \u0026 \\textbf{Transported Metabolites} \u0026 \\textbf{Internal Metabolites/Regulators} \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  mouth \u0026 salivary amylase \u0026 starch, maltose, triolein, arachin \u0026 -- \\\\                            \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  stomach \u0026 gastric amylase, pepsin \u0026 starch, maltose, arachin, polypeptide \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  small intestine \u0026 pancreatic amylase, trypsin, lipase \u0026 starch, maltose, polypeptide, peptide chain, triolein, DAG, MAG, oleic acid \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  small intestine lining \u0026 maltose hydrolysis, peptidase, mixed micelle formation \u0026 maltose, glucose, peptide chain, glutamic acid, bile salt, micelle \u0026 MAG, oleic acid \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  enterocytes \u0026 chylomicron production \u0026 micelle, chylomicron \u0026 -- \\\\     \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  pancreas \u0026 insulin production \u0026 insulin \u0026 -- \\\\           \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  liver \u0026 bile salt production \u0026 bile salt \u0026 -- \\\\                                \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  blood \u0026 lipoprotein lipase, glucose uptake regulator \u0026 glucose, insulin, chylomicrons, oleic acid, glycerol, glutamic acid \u0026 chylomicron remnant \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  cytosol \u0026 glycolysis, acyl-CoA synthase \u0026 glucose, pyruvate, oleic acid, fatty acyl-CoA \u0026 G6P, F6P, F16BP, 3PG, PEP \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  mitochondrial matrix \u0026 alanine amino transferase, TCA cycle, fatty acid oxidation \u0026 pyruvate, glutamic acid, fatty acyl-CoA, alanine, $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate \u0026 GTP, acetyl-CoA \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  mitochondrial inner membrane \u0026 electron transport chain \u0026 NADH, FADH$_2$, ATP \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  \\end{tabularx} \n      \\begin{tablenotes}\n      \\small\n      \\item Note: The term \"organ\" was applied to describe a general area, or set of areas, within the metabolic pipeline. Not all items categorized here, such as blood, or the cytosol, meet the typical definition of organ.\n    \\end{tablenotes}\n  \\end{threeparttable}\n\\end{table}                                                           \n\nThe organs in \\autoref{tab:organ-metabolizers} were organized in a directional, pseudo-acyclic graph structure. Such that, in each simulation time step, an organ receives input metabolites from in its predecessor (for example, the small intestine takes maltose from the stomach). After processing, the organ outputs relevant substrates downstream (i.e. the small intestine outputs glucose to the intestinal lining). This graph structure is demonstrated in \\autoref{fig:graph-view}.\n\n\\begin{figure}\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\linewidth]{graph-view.png}\n    \\caption{Directional graph view of flow of metabolites between organs, including key regulators and metabolites passed through the pipeline}\n    \\label{fig:graph-view}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThis graph-based pipeline is not fully acyclic because the blood acts as an input, output, and regulatory aid for other organs. In this model, for example, the pancreas outputs insulin to the bloodstream. However, it also must access the quantities (e.g. input) of glucose and amino acids within the blood to produce the correct amount of insulin \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}.\n\nEach listed organ was treated as a fully isolated node, which only receives inputs as discrete events. No continuous passage of substrates occured in the model.\n\n\\subsection{Declarative Model Organization}\nWithin code, the model was designed to have a declarative structure, such that functioning code also logically outlined the organization of the human body. Each organ was represented as an object that contained its metabolic agents and rate-limiting functions (\\autoref{fig:code-a}). Each metabolic agent contained a list of its substrates and regulators (\\autoref{fig:code-b}). To create the graph structure, organs were placed within nodes, alongside edges including output metabolites and their destinations (\\autoref{fig:code-c}). \n\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n     \\centering\n     % --- First Row ---\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{organ.png}\n         \\caption{organelle with metabolic processes}\n         \\label{fig:code-a}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n     \\hfill % This pushes the two images to the outer edges\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{metabolic-process.png}\n         \\caption{metabolic process with substrates}\n         \\label{fig:code-b}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n\n     \\vspace{0.5cm} % Adds vertical space between the rows\n\n     % --- Second Row ---\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.96\\textwidth} % You can adjust the width (e.g., 0.7 or 1.0)\n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{organ-node.png}\n         \\caption{edge connecting outputs of organelle to another organelle}\n         \\label{fig:code-c}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n     \n     \\caption{Example, declarative instantiations of physical metabolic organization within code, using the mitochondria (a) , action of alanine amino-transferase (b), and passage of NADH and FADH2 to the inner mitochondrial membrane (c) }\n     \\label{fig:three_images_layout}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Dynamics}\nThe simulation executed in discrete time steps of one minute. In simulation time, each time step took approximately 0.1 seconds. This step size was chosen because all reviewed publications of metabolism kinetics expressed rates in minutes.\n\nIn each time step, the above organ graph was traversed in a breadth-first search. In the first time step, initial peanut macronutrients (\\autoref{sec:micronutrient-inputs}) were input directly to the mouth. Then, the mouth executed kinetics for all contained metabolic agents (none), and placed destination organs for all output edges (only the stomach) into a queue. The next organ-node was removed from the queue, its metabolic agents executed, and destination organs added to the queue. This process iterated until all organs had been visited.\n\nFor all future time steps, the above process repeated, starting with the mouth. However, nutrients were only input into the mouth in the first time step, modeling a single mouthful of peanuts ingested all at once.\n\n\\section{Explanation of Mathematical Models}\nFrom reviewed kinetics publications, first-order and pseudo-first-order \\autocite{noauthor_281_2013} exponential models were common. Others presented a form of the Michaelis-Menten equation, a popular representation for enzymatic kinetics \\autocite{srinivasan_guide_2022}. Ten such models were employed within the simulation. Most models were adopted assuming that the reaction would never be limited by availability of the enzyme, but only the concentration of substrate.\n\nNot all reactions throughout the simulation used a tested model from the literature. Instead, some included naive approaches to facilitate demonstration of the end-to-end pipeline. \\autoref{tab:metabolizer-kinetics} summarizes the substrates, enzymes, and kinetics equation type for each metabolic process. Below, the formulation of each process in the table is detailed.\n\nAll reaction models were solved numerically using a first-order, ordinary differential equations solver (see \\nameref{sec:software-design}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]                                                                                          \n  \\centering                                                                                                                  \n  \\caption{Metabolic Process, Metabolites, and Kinetic Equations}                                                                  \n  \\label{tab:metabolizer-kinetics}                                                                                        \n  \\small % Highly recommended for this table due to the length of the glycolysis row\n  \\begin{tabularx}{\\textwidth}{|l|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|}                                                                                               \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  \\textbf{Metabolizer} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolites} \u0026 \\textbf{Equation Type} \u0026 \\textbf{Source}\\\\                                                                                                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                \n  salivary amylase \u0026 starch $\\rightarrow$ maltose \u0026 first-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}\\\\                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  gastric amylase \u0026 starch $\\rightarrow$ maltose \u0026 first-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012} \\\\                                                                                                                     \n  \\hline                                                                                                                        \n  pepsin \u0026 arachin $\\rightarrow$ polypeptide \u0026 first-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022} \\\\                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  stomach emptying regulator \u0026 starch, maltose, polypeptide, triolein \u0026 exponential power-law \u0026 \\autocite{siegel_biphasic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}\\\\                                                                                                 \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  pancreatic amylase \u0026 starch $\\rightarrow$ maltose \u0026 first-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012} \\\\                                                                                                                      \n  \\hline                                                                                                                       \n  trypsin \u0026 polypeptide $\\rightarrow$ peptide chain \u0026 zero-order \u0026 \\autocite{schwert_kinetics_1949}\\\\                                                           \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  lipase \u0026 triolein $\\rightarrow$ DAG $\\rightarrow$ MAG $\\rightarrow$ oleic acid \u0026 sequential first-order \u0026 \\autocite{lykidis_kinetics_1995}\\\\                                                                                  \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  membrane maltose hydrolysis \u0026 maltose $\\rightarrow$ glucose \u0026 Michaelis-Menten \u0026 \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_2021}\\\\                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                \n  peptidase \u0026 peptide chain $\\rightarrow$ glutamic acid \u0026 first-order \\\\                                                   \n  \\hline                                                                                                                  \n  mixed micelle former \u0026 MAG + oleic acid + bile salt $\\rightarrow$ micelle \u0026 limiting reactant \\\\                                                                                             \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  chylomicron producer \u0026 micelle $\\rightarrow$ chylomicron \u0026 first-order \\\\                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  bile salt producer \u0026 $\\rightarrow$ bile salt \u0026 zero-order \\\\                                                              \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  insulin production \u0026 glucose + glutamic acid $\\rightarrow$ insulin \u0026 linear with feedback \u0026 \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}\\\\                                                                                                     \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  lipoprotein lipase \u0026 chylomicron $\\rightarrow$ chylomicron remnant + oleic acid + glycerol \u0026 zero-order \\\\                                                                                       \n  \\hline                                                                                                                 \n  glucose uptake regulator \u0026 glucose, insulin \u0026 Michaelis-Menten + Hill \u0026 \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_2016}\\\\                                                                                                                          \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  insulin breakdown \u0026 insulin $\\rightarrow$ \u0026 first-order decay \u0026 \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}\\\\                                                            \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  glycolysis \u0026 glucose $\\rightarrow$ G6P $\\rightarrow$ F6P $\\rightarrow$ F16BP $\\rightarrow$ 3PG $\\rightarrow$ PEP $\\rightarrow$ pyruvate \u0026 Michaelis-Menten + Hill \u0026 \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}\\\\                                                                                                        \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  acyl-CoA synthase \u0026 oleic acid $\\rightarrow$ fatty acyl-CoA \u0026 first-order \\\\                                                                                                                      \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                            \n  alanine amino transferase \u0026 glutamic acid + pyruvate $\\leftrightarrow$ $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate + alanine \u0026 first-order \\\\                                                                          \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  TCA cycle \u0026 pyruvate, acetyl-CoA, $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate $\\rightarrow$ NADH, FADH$_2$, GTP \u0026 first-order \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                                                              \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  fatty acid oxidation \u0026 fatty acyl-CoA $\\rightarrow$ acetyl-CoA + NADH + FADH$_2$ \u0026 first-order \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  electron transport chain \u0026 NADH + FADH$_2$ $\\rightarrow$ ATP \u0026 Michaelis-Menten \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                  \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  \\end{tabularx}                                                                                                               \n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Mouth}\nThe mouth was assumed to fully empty its contents into the stomach within a single, one-minute time step. The presence of the esophagus between the mouth and stomach was omitted.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amylase}\n\\label{sec:amylase}\nFor simplicity, starch is treated as 100 percent amylose, which is converted to maltose by amylolysis. However, real starch metabolism would also include a pathway to catabolize contained amylopectin \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Real amylolysis would also produce maltotriose and α-limit dextrins \\autocite{roberts_mechanism_1960, butterworth_enzyme_2022}. \n\nThe mouth contains salivary amylase, and other types of amylase are included in organs below. All amylase reactions were modeled after the same kinetics for pancreatic amylase, a first-order exponential with a rate constant of 0.03 (or starch half-life of 23 minutes) \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Because a model of amylase action specifically on peanuts was not available, the results provided by Butterworth et al. (2012) for beans were selected. Peanuts and beans are in the same legume family, and share similarities in macronutrients \\autocite{toomer_nutritional_2018}.\n\n\\subsection{Stomach}\n\\subsubsection{Gastric Amylase}\nThe same model presented in \\autoref{sec:amylase} acted within the stomach on remaining starch passed to the stomach from the mouth.\n\n\\subsubsection{Pepsin}\nAs no protein catabolism was considered within the mouth, all proteins (arachins) were passed directly to the stomach. Pepsin converted arachin into arachin polypeptides. As a proxy for this reaction, a model fitting the hydrolysis of milk casein by pepsin was used, with a first-order exponential rate constant of 0.08 (protein half life of approximately 9 minutes) \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}.\n\nEffects on the reaction from pepsin denaturation were not considered. Though, other models have considered this effect, particularly for increased denaturation at pH levels over 5.0 \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}. Stomach acidity may approach this level after ingestion of a meal \\autocite{noauthor_regulation_2006}.\n\nOur model further assumed that pepsin was already active in the stomach upon food ingestion, ignoring preliminary activation of pepsinogen to pepsin by hydrochloric acid in the within the stomach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Stomach Empyting Regulator}\nAfter nutrients in the stomach have undergone noted enzymatic reactions and formed into acid chyme, they are emptied into the duodenum of the small intestine. This step is tightly controlled via the pyloric sphincter, at a potential rate of 2-4 kilocalories per minute \\autocite{kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nOur simulation used a power exponential function reviewed by Kong et al. (2008). This model resembled other first-order exponential models discussed, but included a power constant to account for an initial lag period in the emptying of solid foods. With this model, 50 percent of solids were expected to be emptied in approximately 94 minutes, and completely emptied in 2 or more hours \\autocite{siegel_biphasic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nWhile this model did not differentiate between distinct macronutrients,  as chyme passes to the small intestine in real metabolism, a striation of chyme would be expected, with emulsified fats on top. Our simulation did not account for such separation, and instead assumed that all nutrients were distributed and emptied in equal proportion.\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine}\n\\subsubsection{Pancreatic Amylase}\nThe model for pancreatic amylase matched details of \\autoref{sec:amylase}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Trypsin}\nTo simplify, trypsin was the only enzyme considered in converting polypeptides within the small intestine into small peptide chains. However, a more robust model should consider also the presence of chymotripsin and carboxypeptidase in this step.\n\nOur model based the catabolic rate of polypeptides on findings by Schwert and Eisenberg (1949) of the action of trypsin on benzoyl-L-argininamide. While the authors noted that the reaction theoretically requires higher-order modeling due to varying levels of competitive inhibition depending on substrate concenration, experimental results followed a zero-order (linear) hydrolysis rate of 1.1e-3 millimoles per minute of polypeptides \\autocite{schwert_kinetics_1949}. This same zero-order model was selected for simulation.\n\n\\subsubsection{Lipase}\nLipases which catalyze conversion of triglycerides to di- and mono-glycerides are present in the mouth, stomach, small intestine, and intestinal lining. Because most lipolysis occurs within the small intestine after emulsification via bile salts (not available to the stomach or mouth), only lipase within the small intestine (pancreatic lipase) was considered.\n\nThe selected model of pancreatic lipase presented distinct first-order reactions for hydrolysis of triolein to 1,2-diolein, and then 1,2-diolein to 2-monolein. Each reaction was dependent on the initial concentration of triolein, and rate constants for both substeps. Dioglycerides were catabolized nearly three times faster than trioglycerides \\autocite{lykidis_kinetics_1995}.\n\n\\subsection{Small-Intestinal-Lining}\n\\subsubsection{Maltose Hydrolysis}\nMaltose resulting from amylolysis earlier in the organ pipeline was hydrolized to glucose. This reaction is catalized by maltase. A Michaelis-Menten equation model was selected, whose numerical parameters were derived from experiments with rat small jejenums \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_2021}. \n\nTo apply this model to humans, a typical human small intestine length \\autocite{fish_physiology_2025} and fed intestinal fluid volume \\autocite{schiller_intestinal_2005} replaced values used for rats.\n\n\\subsubsection{Peptidase}\nPeptidase catalyzes the hydrolysis reaction converting peptide chains into individual amino acids (all assumed to be glutamic acid). \n\nThe rate for this step is not sourced. For demonstration, it is assumed to be first-order, catabolizing 20 percent of existing small peptide chains during each time step.\n\n\\subsubsection{Mixed Micelle Formation}\nTo enter the enterocytes, monoglycerides and free fatty acids within the small intestinal lining first form emulsified mixed micelles with bile salts. \n\nKinetics for this process were not sourced. A naive model was implemented, in which a single mole of monolein, two moles or oleic acid, and one mole of bile coalesce to form a mole of micelle. The lowest-quantity substrate was selected as the limiting reactant in each time step, and the reaction consumed 50 percent of the limiting reactant, alongside corresponding proportions of both other substrates.\n\n\\subsubsection{Chylomicron Production}\nAfter passing into the enterocytes via mixed micelles, glycerides and fatty acids form into chylomicrons, which are water-soluble, and hence may enter the bloodstream.\n\nEnterocytes were modeled as a distinct organ in the simulation to emphasize this step.\n\nFor simplicitly, 50 percent of micelles from the interstinal lining per time step were assumed to eventually produce chylomicrons, with all intermediate steps omitted.  \n\n\n\\subsection{Liver}\n\\subsubsection{Bile Salt Production}\nBile salt was modeled to be injected directly to the small intestine from the liver. However, in reality, bile would first be stored within the gall bladder, and later delivered via the bile duct.\n\nOur modeled liver produced and transported 1mmol of bile salt per time step.\n\n\\subsection{Pancreas}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Production}\n\\label{sec:insulin-production}\nInsulin production by the pancreas, and secretion to the bloodstream, relied on a model with both plasma glucose, and plasma amino acids, as regulators. To determine the needed level of insulin in a time step, the model assigned linear coefficients to current levels of glucose, amino acids, and insulin. Those coefficients were applied to the differences between current levels and assumed base levels, such that a higher change from base levels corresponded to a higher change in insulin.\n\\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}.\n\nWe adopted this model by communicating calculated values of glucose and glutamic acid within the simulated bloodstream to the pancreas node, which were used as parameters in the provided linear equation.\n\n\\subsection{Bloodstream}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Degradation}\nThe same model used in \\autoref{sec:insulin-production} accounted for insulin degradation within the blood via first-order feedback. Assuming a steady-state average insulin level $I_b$ of approximately 7.5 $mU/ml$, and a rate coefficient $k_I$ of 0.14, insulin in the next time step was denatured by \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}:\n\n\\[Insulin(t) -\u003d k_I * (Insulin(t - 1) - I_b)\\]\n\nIn this manner, insulin was expected to naturally degrade to its base level in steady state. The specific kinetics of the insulin-degrading enzyme, which contribue to an expected insulin half-life of 4-6 minutes \\autocite{noauthor_chapter_2009}, were not included in this approach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glucose Uptake Regulator}\nInsulin levels in the blood affect the rate at which glucose is absorbed into cells. Passage of glucose from the blood across the cell membrane was implemented as a one-way edge between the blood and cytosol nodes. Glucose uptake regulation was implemented as a rate limiter for this edge.\n\nEstimated parameters were selected from a Michaelis-Menten equation of glucose uptake, fit to glucose and insulin readings of human adults. This equation modeled the maximum glucose uptake per unit time as a Hill function \\autocite{goutelle_hill_2008} of insulin concentration at the active site \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_2016}. \n\n\\subsection{Within cells}\nDistinct areas of cells, including the cytosol, mitochondrial matrix, and inner mitochondrial membrane, were modeled as though the volume of participating cells of the body were combined into a single cell, with equal distribution of nutrients from the blood to all areas. This volume was visualized as single, pseudo-organ nodes for each area.\n\nTypes of cells were not differentiated, and a total count of 30.00e12 cells participating in glycolysis and energy generation was assumed \\autocite{hatton_human_2023}\n\n\\subsubsection{Glycolysis - Cytosol}\nGlucose which enters the cell cytosol undergoes glycolysis, with the final output of pyruvate. The simulation assumed only aerobic conditions, and, hence, that zero pyruvate was ever later reduced to lactic acid. \n\nFurther, glycolysis, and other below catabolic processes within the cells, treated adenosine-triphosphate (ATP) levels as effectively unlimited. Their availability was not considered for substrate inputs, nor outputs (excluding the ETC.)\n\nThe selected model considered seven different intermediate substrates, in order of appearance in glycolysis: glucose, glucose-6-phosphate (G6P), fructose-6-phosphate (F6P), fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (F16P) 3-phosphoglycerate (3PG), phosphenolpyruvate (PEP), and pyruvate. The rate of change of each was modeled with Michaelis-Menten and Hill kinetics as a function of other intermediates \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}.\n\n\\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} shows highly-simplified relationships between each of these substrates. While the table omits feedback effects on particular glycolytic enzymes, expected examples of feed-forward and -backward regulation are present. For example, F16P increases the later production of PEP from 3PG (hence increasing PEP and decreasing 3PG) by allosterically activating pyruvate kinase, which catalyzes this reaction \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}. As a backwards-feedback example, G6P\u0027s inhibition of hexokinase, which catalyzes conversion of glucose to G6P, is encoded in \\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} as \"self-regulation\".\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Glycolysis ODE Metabolite Dependencies}\n\\label{tab:glycolysis-dependencies}\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.3} % Better spacing for readability\n\\begin{NiceTabular}{llc}[\n    code-before \u003d {\n        % This tells LaTeX: Start at row 2, alternate gray and white, \n        % but only apply it to columns 2 and 3.\n        \\rowcolors{2}{gray!10}{white}[cols\u003d{2-3}]\n    }\n]\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metabolite} \u0026 \\textbf{Dependency} \u0026 \\textbf{Effect} \\\\\n\\midrule\n\nGLC (Intracellular Glucose) \u0026 GLC \u0026 $\\pm$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{3-1}{G6P (Glucose-6-Phosphate)} \n    \u0026 GLC \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{2-1}{F6P (Fructose-6-Phosphate)} \n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{2-1}{F16BP (Fructose-1,6-Bisphosphate)} \n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{3-1}{3PG (3-Phosphoglycerate)} \n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{3-1}{PEP (Phosphoenolpyruvate)} \n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 $-$  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\n\\Block{2-1}{Pyruvate} \n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{NiceTabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nOur simulation lacked key glycolysis regulation steps due to omission of non-substrate regulators. In particular, no enzymes were regulated by presence of energy-yielding molecules, such as ATP, adenosine di-phosphate (ADP), and adenosine-mono-phosphate (AMP).\n\n\\subsubsection{Fatty Acid Oxidation -- Mitochondrial Matrix}\nBelow reactions within the mitochondrial matrix are implemented naively in that input from previous steps were assumed to be converted directly into energy-yielding molecules. However, net quantities of energy-yielding molecules output were expected to be realistic.\n\nTo retain a pool of cycle inputs for visualization, only 75 percent were processed in each time step for fatty acid oxidation, conversion of amino acids to alanine, the TCA Cycle.\n\n1mol of Fatty acyl-CoA produced a mol of acetyl-CoA based on the length of the fatty acid carbon chain:\n\n\\[Acetyl-CoA \u003d N_C / 2 - 1\\]\n\nin addition 1mol NADH and 1mol FADH2 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}.\n\nAs oleic acid has a carbon length of 18 \\autocite{pubchem_oleic_nodate}, each step produced 8mol Acetyl-CoA per mol of oleic acid.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amino Acid Catabolism - Mitochondrial Matrix}\nGlutamic acid and pyruvate react to form a-ketoglutarate and alanine, when catalyzed by alanine-aminotransferase \\autocite{lenartowicz_significance_1988}. a-ketoglutarate can then enter the TCA cycle \\autoref{sec:tca}\n\nOur model assumed that glutamic acid would always be the limiting substrate of this reaction, such that, in each time step, 75 percent of contained glutamic acid was consumed, along with an equal number of mols of pyruvate.\n\n\n\\subsubsection{TCA Cycle - Mitochondrial Matrix}\n\\label{sec:tca}\nEach mol of acetyl-CoA and pyruvate (intermediate conversion to oxaloacetate omitted) produced 3mol NADH, 1mol FADH2, and 1mol guanosine-triphosphate (GTP). Ketogluratate produced 2mol NADH, 1mol FADH2, and 1mol GTP. \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\n\nThe mitochondrial matrix included two processes with the same input substrate -- both amino acid catabolism and TCA required pyruvate. Distribution of pyruvate to these two processes was implemented via a simple, ordered list. First, all available glutamate was converted. Then, all remaining pyruvate was passed to the numerical solver for TCA.\n\n\\subsubsection{ETC - Mitochondrial Inner Membrane}\nThe ETC model assumed that 2mol ATP were produced per mol NADH, and 1.5mol per mol FADH2. The maximum production rate was ATP 100 molecules per minute, per ATP-synthase \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. Total production considered total number of active synthases as a product of synthases per mitochondria, mitochondria per cell, and total cells (\\nameref{sec:physical-constants}).\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\subsection{Strengths}\nThis simulation presented a unified set of researched reaction kinetics. While such kinetics of major metabolic steps are accessible in the literature, relatively little has been published combining many together to demonstrate larger-scale effects in the body.\n\nThis work closely coupled demonstrative code with an intuitive organization of metabolism, and scientific sources. Based on a limited search, such design and prioritization of code accessibility appear to be novel in biochemical kinetics research.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\nAt the time of writing, this simulation is a prototype intended to demonstrate knowledge at an introductory graduate level. It included steps which are known to be based on incorrect assumptions, and omitted important steps, particularly in regulation. As the software had not been a part of a rigorous review, the code likely contained further defects.\n\nModeling for this simulation was not based on a thorough literature review. High-level model flow was taken from course lecture content \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}, and more specific sources for individual reactions were selected on their strengths of easily accessible, and relevant parameterized models. Projected accuracy within the overall metabolic pipeline was not the highest priority.\n\n\\subsection{Future work}\nPossible improvements noted above are strong candidates to improve robustness of the presented simulation. To remove overly-strong assumptions, a literature review should be conducted to better select models for each interaction. More recently-research models should be added, including realistic models of the transfer of metabolites between organs and organelles (not considered in the current simulation).\n\nCode should be reviewed and improved for maintainability by multiple investigators. Following, it can be considered as a tool to continue to closely map intuitive, physical body structure to functioning code which demonstrates the current state of the literature. Such a tool will be useful to unify metabolism research, and identify gaps.\n\n\nNo anabolic processes have yet been included, such as transportation of glycerol from triglyceride catabolism to the liver for either gluconeogenesis for fatty acid reconstruction. Addition of these processes would augment the demonstrative capability of this simulation as a learning tool. Further, the developed code base is equipped to support new such transports and processes with minimal time investment.\n\n\n\\section{Appendix I: Physical Constants} \n\\label{sec:physical-constants}\n\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.4}\n\\small\n% Define a wrapping column with left alignment\n\\newcolumntype{Y}{\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X}\n\n\\begin{xltabular}{\\textwidth}{Y l l Y l}\n    % --- Table Caption and Header ---\n    \\caption{Physical Constants Used in Metabolism Simulation} \\label{tab:physical-constants} \\\\\n    \\toprule\n    \\textbf{Constant} \u0026 \\textbf{Value} \u0026 \\textbf{Units} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolic Process(es)} \u0026 \\textbf{Source} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    \\endfirsthead\n\n    % --- Header for subsequent pages ---\n    \\multicolumn{5}{c}{\\textit{Table \\thetable{} continued from previous page}} \\\\\n    \\toprule\n    \\textbf{Constant} \u0026 \\textbf{Value} \u0026 \\textbf{Units} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolic Process(es)} \u0026 \\textbf{Source} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    \\endhead\n\n    % --- Footer for pages before the last ---\n    \\midrule\n    \\multicolumn{5}{r}{\\textit{Continued on next page}} \\\\\n    \\endfoot\n\n    % --- Final Footer ---\n    \\bottomrule\n    \\endlastfoot\n\n    % --- Table Body ---\n    \\rowcolor{gray!25} \\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textbf{Fundamental Constants}} \\\\\n    AVOGADRO \u0026 $6.022 \\times 10^{23}$ \u0026 molecules/mol \u0026 Electron Transport Chain \u0026 -- \\\\\n    \\midrule\n\n    \\rowcolor{gray!25} \\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textbf{Human Body Parameters}} \\\\\n    \\seqsplit{AVERAGE\\_HUMAN\\_BODY\\_SURFACE\\_AREA} \u0026 1.79 \u0026 m$^2$ \u0026 Glucose Uptake Regulation \u0026 \\autocite{sacco_average_2010} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n\n    \\rowcolor{gray!25} \\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textbf{Blood Parameters}} \\\\\n    TOTAL\\_LITERS \u0026 5.0 \u0026 L \u0026 Glycolysis, Insulin, Blood Init \u0026 \\autocite{sharma_physiology_2025} \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} basalGlucoseMgDl \u0026 90 \u0026 mg/dL \u0026 Insulin Production \u0026 \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022} \\\\\n    basalInsulin \u0026 7.5 \u0026 $\\mu$U/mL \u0026 Insulin Production, Blood Init \u0026 \\autocite{schrank_proposal_2024} \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} basalBCAA \u0026 475 \u0026 $\\mu$mol/L \u0026 Insulin Production, Blood Init \u0026 -- \\\\\n    \\midrule\n\n    \\rowcolor{gray!25} \\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textbf{Cell Parameters}} \\\\\n    TOTAL\\_COUNT \u0026 $3.0 \\times 10^{13}$ \u0026 cells \u0026 Glycolysis, ETC \u0026 \\autocite{hatton_human_2023} \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} SINGLE\\_VOLUME \u0026 $3.04 \\times 10^{-12}$ \u0026 L/cell \u0026 Glycolysis \u0026 \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014} \\\\\n    \\seqsplit{MITOCHONDRIA\\_PER\\_CELL} \u0026 1000 \u0026 -- \u0026 Electron Transport Chain \u0026 \\autocite{cole_evolution_2016} \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} \\seqsplit{ACTIVE\\_ATP\\_SYNTHASE\\_PER\\_MITOCHONDRIA} \u0026 1000 \u0026 -- \u0026 Electron Transport Chain \u0026 \\autocite{fahimi_power_2021} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n\n    \\rowcolor{gray!25} \\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textbf{Molar Masses}} \\\\\n    amlyose \u0026 200,000 \u0026 g/mol \u0026 Salivary/Pancreatic Amylase, Input \u0026 noauthor_amylose_nodate \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} MALTOSE \u0026 342.30 \u0026 g/mol \u0026 Salivary/Pancreatic Amylase \u0026 \\autocite{pubchem_maltose_nodate} \\\\\n    GLUCOSE \u0026 180.16 \u0026 g/mol \u0026 Insulin Production \u0026 \\autocite{pubchem_alpha-d-glucose_nodate} \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} ARACHIN \u0026 490,000 \u0026 g/mol \u0026 Pepsin, Input \u0026 \\autocite{basha_polypeptide_1981} \\\\\n    ARACHIN\\_POLYPEPTIDE \u0026 68,000 \u0026 g/mol \u0026 Pepsin, Trypsin \u0026 \\autocite{basha_polypeptide_1981} \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} SMALL\\_PEPTIDE\u0026 680 \u0026 g/mol \u0026 Trypsin, Peptidase \u0026 no source, assumed 1 percent of polypeptide \\\\\n    GLUTAMIC\\_ACID \u0026 147.134 \u0026 g/mol \u0026 Peptidase \u0026 \\autocite{pubchem_l-glutamic_nodate} \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} TRIOLEIN \u0026 885.4 \u0026 g/mol \u0026 Input \u0026 \\autocite{pubchem_triolein_nodate} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n\n    \\rowcolor{gray!25} \\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textbf{Fatty Acid Properties}} \\\\\n    OLEIC\\_ACID (chain length) \u0026 18 \u0026 carbons \u0026 Fatty Acid $\\beta$-Oxidation \u0026 \\autocite{settaluri_peanuts_2012} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n\n    \\rowcolor{gray!25} \\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\textbf{Maltose Hydrolysis Parameters}} \\\\\n    \\rowcolor{gray!10} LUMEN\\_VOLUME\\_LITERS \u0026 0.054 \u0026 L \u0026 Membrane Maltose Hydrolysis \u0026 \\autocite{schiller_intestinal_2005} \\\\\n\\end{xltabular}\n\n\\section{Appendix II: Software Design Details}\n\\label{sec:software-design}\nThe simulation data modeling was implemented using the Kotlin Multiplatform \\autocite{noauthor_kotlin_nodate} tool-set, popular for developing applications which compile for multiple runtimes. The simulation visualization was created with the corresponding UI framework, Kotlin Compose \\autocite{noauthor_get_nodate}. The simulation was built and packaged using \\autocite{noauthor_gradle_2025}, developed using the IntelliJ IDEA: Ultimate Edition\n\\autocite{noauthor_leading_nodate}, and tested on a 2024 Apple Mac mini with Apple M4 CPU, and 24 gigabytes of RAM. While the simulation application has been made available for both macOS and Windows distributions, it has not yet been tested on Windows. \n\nKinetics were numerically solved using a first-order differential equations solver from the Apache Maths Commons Java library \\autocite{noauthor_math_nodate}. It was configured to use the Dormand-Prince 853 integrator with default parameters \\autocite{noauthor_dormandprince853integrator_nodate}. Optimization of these parameters would likely improve accuracy of simulation performance. This is a candidate for future work.\n\nAll code and build artifacts from this work are publically available on GitHub: \n\nhttps://github.com/jsharp4/PeanutsMetabolismSimulator\n\nClaude Code was used via a command line terminal during project development. See \\nameref{sec:ai}.\n\n\\section{Appendix III: A.I. Usage}\n\\label{sec:ai}\nCommercially available A.I. tools were used to assist with the construction of this simulation, and for formatting aspects of this paper. All suggested output was verified by this author. All ideas within the paper, and in simulation data design, are cited, or originally of this author.\n\nClaude Code was used for code refactoring, assistance with triaging issues within the application