      "text": "Here is the corrected and updated LaTeX document. \n\n**Changes made:**\n*   **Grammar/Spelling:** Fixed \"occured\" to \"occurred,\" \"publically\" to \"publicly,\" sentence fragments in the graph structure description, and \"concenration\" to \"concentration.\"\n*   **Notation:** Updated scientific notation from E-notation (e.g., $3.0e13$) to formal LaTeX math notation ($3.0 \\times 10^{13}$).\n*   **Greek Characters:** Ensured $\\alpha$ (alpha) and $\\beta$ (beta) render correctly using math mode.\n*   **Natural Language:** Converted all-caps and underscored variable names (like `AVERAGE_HUMAN_BODY_SURFACE_AREA`) into natural title-case English.\n*   **Formatting:** Standardized \"Mouth,\" \"Stomach,\" etc., in tables to start with capital letters.\n*   **Citations:** Fixed a missing `\\autocite` command in the Molar Masses table and ensured all keys remain functionally linked.\n\n```latex\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[table]{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\setlength{\\parindent}{0pt}\n\\setlength{\\parskip}{1em}\n\\usepackage[a4paper, margin\u003d1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{nicematrix}\n\\newcommand{\\var}[1]{\\texttt{\\seqsplit{#1}}} \n\\usepackage{seqsplit} \n\\usepackage{xltabular}\n\\usepackage{threeparttable}\n\n\\title{Discretized, First-Order-Kinetics Simulation of the Catabolism Steps of Major Macronutrients of a Peanut in the Human Body}\n\\author{Jon Sharp \\\\\n    jon.sharp@ucdconnect.ie \\\\\n    UCD Student Number: 25278680\n    }\n\\date{December 22, 2025}\n\n\\usepackage[style\u003dauthoryear,backend\u003dbiber]{biblatex}\n\n\\addbibresource{references.bib}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle \n\n\\section{Introduction}\nThis paper presents a software simulation prototype of the catabolism of key peanut macronutrients within the human body. The simulation begins with chosen quantities of starch, proteins, and fats in the mouth. It concludes in the mitochondria, with final outputs of produced ATP or energy equivalents from the Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle and Electron Transport Chain (ETC). The simulation does not include major anabolic steps which influence the fate of peanut nutrients.\n\nA video demo of the simulation is accessible at the following link: \\\\\n\\url{https://drive.google.com/file/d/1iAvv22Su3pxp-vrJf5WH93K_mMKOf0L_/view?usp\u003dsharing}\n\nTo construct this simulation, successful enzymatic reaction rate models have been selected from the literature. Individual catabolic steps are intended to appear realistic. However, the project prioritized learning and qualitative demonstration over quantitative accuracy. As discussed below, strong assumptions made for the simulation model fail to capture the true complexities of metabolism.\n\nFurther sections address simulation construction, models used in each organ and metabolic process, assumptions, and limitations. Most of the theory which influenced simulation design and discussion came from graduate course lecture content \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. This content has been explicitly cited below only in instances where specific numerical values were used in calculations, but it served as the basis for the majority of the content design.\n\n\\section{Simulation Construction}\n\\subsection{Micronutrient Inputs}\n\\label{sec:micronutrient-inputs}\nTo represent a peanut beginning ingestion in the mouth, quantities of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins corresponding to 50 grams of whole peanuts were selected. For simplicity, the model only considered the most prominent of each of these macronutrients in peanuts: starch, oleic acid (monounsaturated) \\autocite{settaluri_peanuts_2012}, and $\\alpha$-arachin \\autocite{cui_peanut_2023}, respectively. All triglycerides were assumed to be triolein.\n\nSee \\nameref{sec:physical-constants} for a complete list of sourced molar masses for these nutrients and other relevant physical constants used in the model.\n\n\\subsection{Organ Pipeline Model}\n\\autoref{tab:organ-metabolizers} shows a list of all considered organs/organelles, alongside each metabolic process within that organ and the processed substrates.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]                                                              \n  \\centering   \n  \\begin{threeparttable}\n  \\caption{Organs, Metabolic Agents, and Metabolites in the Peanut Metabolism Simulator}\n  \\label{tab:organ-metabolizers}                                                \n  \\small\n  \\begin{tabularx}{\\textwidth}{|l|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|}                                          \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  \\textbf{Organ} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolic Agent} \u0026 \\textbf{Transported Metabolites} \u0026 \\textbf{Internal Metabolites/Regulators} \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Mouth \u0026 Salivary amylase \u0026 Starch, maltose, triolein, arachin \u0026 -- \\\\                            \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Stomach \u0026 Gastric amylase, pepsin \u0026 Starch, maltose, arachin, polypeptide \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Small Intestine \u0026 Pancreatic amylase, trypsin, lipase \u0026 Starch, maltose, polypeptide, peptide chain, triolein, DAG, MAG, oleic acid \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Small Intestine Lining \u0026 Maltose hydrolysis, peptidase, mixed micelle formation \u0026 Maltose, glucose, peptide chain, glutamic acid, bile salt, micelle \u0026 MAG, oleic acid \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Enterocytes \u0026 Chylomicron production \u0026 Micelle, chylomicron \u0026 -- \\\\     \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Pancreas \u0026 Insulin production \u0026 Insulin \u0026 -- \\\\           \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Liver \u0026 Bile salt production \u0026 Bile salt \u0026 -- \\\\                                \n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Blood \u0026 Lipoprotein lipase, glucose uptake regulator \u0026 Glucose, insulin, chylomicrons, oleic acid, glycerol, glutamic acid \u0026 Chylomicron remnant \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Cytosol \u0026 Glycolysis, acyl-CoA synthase \u0026 Glucose, pyruvate, oleic acid, fatty acyl-CoA \u0026 G6P, F6P, F16BP, 3PG, PEP \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Mitochondrial Matrix \u0026 Alanine amino transferase, TCA cycle, fatty acid oxidation \u0026 Pyruvate, glutamic acid, fatty acyl-CoA, alanine, $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate \u0026 GTP, acetyl-CoA \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  Mitochondrial Inner Membrane \u0026 Electron transport chain \u0026 NADH, FADH$_2$, ATP \u0026 -- \\\\\n  \\hline                                                                        \n  \\end{tabularx} \n      \\begin{tablenotes}\n      \\small\n      \\item Note: The term \"organ\" was applied to describe a general area, or set of areas, within the metabolic pipeline. Not all items categorized here, such as blood or the cytosol, meet the typical definition of an organ.\n    \\end{tablenotes}\n  \\end{threeparttable}\n\\end{table}                                                           \n\nThe organs in \\autoref{tab:organ-metabolizers} were organized in a directional, pseudo-acyclic graph structure. In each simulation time step, an organ receives input metabolites from its predecessor (for example, the small intestine takes maltose from the stomach). After processing, the organ outputs relevant substrates downstream (i.e., the small intestine outputs glucose to the intestinal lining). This graph structure is demonstrated in \\autoref{fig:graph-view}.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\linewidth]{graph-view.png}\n    \\caption{Directional graph view of flow of metabolites between organs, including key regulators and metabolites passed through the pipeline}\n    \\label{fig:graph-view}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThis graph-based pipeline is not fully acyclic because the blood acts as an input, output, and regulatory aid for other organs. In this model, for example, the pancreas outputs insulin to the bloodstream. However, it also must access the quantities (inputs) of glucose and amino acids within the blood to produce the correct amount of insulin \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}.\n\nEach listed organ was treated as a fully isolated node which only receives inputs as discrete events. No continuous passage of substrates occurred in the model.\n\n\\subsection{Declarative Model Organization}\nWithin the code, the model was designed to have a declarative structure, such that functioning code also logically outlined the organization of the human body. Each organ was represented as an object that contained its metabolic agents and rate-limiting functions (\\autoref{fig:code-a}). Each metabolic agent contained a list of its substrates and regulators (\\autoref{fig:code-b}). To create the graph structure, organs were placed within nodes, alongside edges including output metabolites and their destinations (\\autoref{fig:code-c}). \n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n     \\centering\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{organ.png}\n         \\caption{organelle with metabolic processes}\n         \\label{fig:code-a}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n     \\hfill \n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.48\\textwidth}\n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{metabolic-process.png}\n         \\caption{metabolic process with substrates}\n         \\label{fig:code-b}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n\n     \\vspace{0.5cm} \n\n     \\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.96\\textwidth} \n         \\centering\n         \\includegraphics[width\u003d\\textwidth]{organ-node.png}\n         \\caption{edge connecting outputs of organelle to another organelle}\n         \\label{fig:code-c}\n     \\end{subfigure}\n     \n     \\caption{Example declarative instantiations of physical metabolic organization within code, using the mitochondria (a), the action of alanine aminotransferase (b), and the passage of NADH and FADH$_2$ to the inner mitochondrial membrane (c)}\n     \\label{fig:three_images_layout}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Dynamics}\nThe simulation executed in discrete time steps of one minute. In simulation time, each time step took approximately 0.1 seconds. This step size was chosen because all reviewed publications of metabolism kinetics expressed rates in minutes.\n\nIn each time step, the above organ graph was traversed in a breadth-first search. In the first time step, initial peanut macronutrients (\\autoref{sec:micronutrient-inputs}) were input directly to the mouth. Then, the mouth executed kinetics for all contained metabolic agents (none) and placed destination organs for all output edges (only the stomach) into a queue. The next organ-node was removed from the queue, its metabolic agents executed, and destination organs added to the queue. This process iterated until all organs had been visited.\n\nFor all future time steps, the above process repeated, starting with the mouth. However, nutrients were only input into the mouth in the first time step, modeling a single mouthful of peanuts ingested all at once.\n\n\\section{Explanation of Mathematical Models}\nFrom reviewed kinetics publications, first-order and pseudo-first-order \\autocite{noauthor_281_2013} exponential models were common. Others presented a form of the Michaelis-Menten equation, a popular representation for enzymatic kinetics \\autocite{srinivasan_guide_2022}. Ten such models were employed within the simulation. Most models were adopted assuming that the reaction would never be limited by the availability of the enzyme, but only the concentration of the substrate.\n\nNot all reactions throughout the simulation used a tested model from the literature. Instead, some included naive approaches to facilitate demonstration of the end-to-end pipeline. \\autoref{tab:metabolizer-kinetics} summarizes the substrates, enzymes, and kinetics equation type for each metabolic process. Below, the formulation of each process in the table is detailed.\n\nAll reaction models were solved numerically using a first-order, ordinary differential equations solver (see \\nameref{sec:software-design}).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]                                                                                          \n  \\centering                                                                                                                  \n  \\caption{Metabolic Process, Metabolites, and Kinetic Equations}                                                                  \n  \\label{tab:metabolizer-kinetics}                                                                                        \n  \\small \n  \\begin{tabularx}{\\textwidth}{|l|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X|}                                                                                               \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  \\textbf{Metabolizer} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolites} \u0026 \\textbf{Equation Type} \u0026 \\textbf{Source}\\\\                                                                                                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                \n  Salivary amylase \u0026 Starch $\\rightarrow$ Maltose \u0026 First-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}\\\\                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  Gastric amylase \u0026 Starch $\\rightarrow$ Maltose \u0026 First-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012} \\\\                                                                                                                     \n  \\hline                                                                                                                        \n  Pepsin \u0026 Arachin $\\rightarrow$ Polypeptide \u0026 First-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022} \\\\                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  Stomach emptying regulator \u0026 Starch, maltose, polypeptide, triolein \u0026 Exponential power-law \u0026 \\autocite{siegel_biphasic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}\\\\                                                                                                 \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  Pancreatic amylase \u0026 Starch $\\rightarrow$ Maltose \u0026 First-order exponential \u0026 \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012} \\\\                                                                                                                      \n  \\hline                                                                                                                       \n  Trypsin \u0026 Polypeptide $\\rightarrow$ Peptide chain \u0026 Zero-order \u0026 \\autocite{schwert_kinetics_1949}\\\\                                                           \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  Lipase \u0026 Triolein $\\rightarrow$ DAG $\\rightarrow$ MAG $\\rightarrow$ Oleic acid \u0026 Sequential first-order \u0026 \\autocite{lykidis_kinetics_1995}\\\\                                                                                  \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  Membrane maltose hydrolysis \u0026 Maltose $\\rightarrow$ Glucose \u0026 Michaelis-Menten \u0026 \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_2021}\\\\                                                                                                                \n  \\hline                                                                                                                \n  Peptidase \u0026 Peptide chain $\\rightarrow$ Glutamic acid \u0026 First-order \\\\                                                   \n  \\hline                                                                                                                  \n  Mixed micelle former \u0026 MAG + Oleic acid + Bile salt $\\rightarrow$ Micelle \u0026 Limiting reactant \\\\                                                                                             \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  Chylomicron producer \u0026 Micelle $\\rightarrow$ Chylomicron \u0026 First-order \\\\                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  Bile salt producer \u0026 $\\rightarrow$ Bile salt \u0026 Zero-order \\\\                                                              \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  Insulin production \u0026 Glucose + Glutamic acid $\\rightarrow$ Insulin \u0026 Linear with feedback \u0026 \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}\\\\                                                                                                     \n  \\hline                                                                                                                   \n  Lipoprotein lipase \u0026 Chylomicron $\\rightarrow$ Chylomicron remnant + Oleic acid + Glycerol \u0026 Zero-order \\\\                                                                                       \n  \\hline                                                                                                                 \n  Glucose uptake regulator \u0026 Glucose, insulin \u0026 Michaelis-Menten + Hill \u0026 \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_2016}\\\\                                                                                                                          \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  Insulin breakdown \u0026 Insulin $\\rightarrow$ \u0026 First-order decay \u0026 \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}\\\\                                                            \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  Glycolysis \u0026 Glucose $\\rightarrow$ G6P $\\rightarrow$ F6P $\\rightarrow$ F16BP $\\rightarrow$ 3PG $\\rightarrow$ PEP $\\rightarrow$ Pyruvate \u0026 Michaelis-Menten + Hill \u0026 \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}\\\\                                                                                                        \n  \\hline                                                                                                                    \n  Acyl-CoA synthase \u0026 Oleic acid $\\rightarrow$ Fatty acyl-CoA \u0026 First-order \\\\                                                                                                                      \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                            \n  Alanine aminotransferase \u0026 Glutamic acid + Pyruvate $\\leftrightarrow$ $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate + Alanine \u0026 First-order \\\\                                                                          \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  TCA cycle \u0026 Pyruvate, acetyl-CoA, $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate $\\rightarrow$ NADH, FADH$_2$, GTP \u0026 First-order \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                                                              \n  \\hline                                                                                                                                                                                          \n  Fatty acid oxidation \u0026 Fatty acyl-CoA $\\rightarrow$ Acetyl-CoA + NADH + FADH$_2$ \u0026 First-order \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                                                                         \n  \\hline                                                                                                                      \n  Electron transport chain \u0026 NADH + FADH$_2$ $\\rightarrow$ ATP \u0026 Michaelis-Menten \u0026 \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}\\\\                                                                                                                  \n  \\hline                                                                                                                     \n  \\end{tabularx}                                                                                                               \n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Mouth}\nThe mouth was assumed to fully empty its contents into the stomach within a single, one-minute time step. The presence of the esophagus between the mouth and stomach was omitted.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amylase}\n\\label{sec:amylase}\nFor simplicity, starch is treated as 100 percent amylose, which is converted to maltose by amylolysis. However, real starch metabolism would also include a pathway to catabolize contained amylopectin \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Real amylolysis would also produce maltotriose and $\\alpha$-limit dextrins \\autocite{roberts_mechanism_1960, butterworth_enzyme_2022}. \n\nThe mouth contains salivary amylase, and other types of amylase are included in organs below. All amylase reactions were modeled after the same kinetics for pancreatic amylase, a first-order exponential with a rate constant of 0.03 (or starch half-life of 23 minutes) \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Because a model of amylase action specifically on peanuts was not available, the results provided by Butterworth et al. (2012) for beans were selected. Peanuts and beans are in the same legume family and share similarities in macronutrients \\autocite{toomer_nutritional_2018}.\n\n\\subsection{Stomach}\n\\subsubsection{Gastric Amylase}\nThe same model presented in \\autoref{sec:amylase} acted within the stomach on remaining starch passed from the mouth.\n\n\\subsubsection{Pepsin}\nAs no protein catabolism was considered within the mouth, all proteins (arachins) were passed directly to the stomach. Pepsin converted arachin into arachin polypeptides. As a proxy for this reaction, a model fitting the hydrolysis of milk casein by pepsin was used, with a first-order exponential rate constant of 0.08 (protein half-life of approximately 9 minutes) \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}.\n\nEffects on the reaction from pepsin denaturation were not considered. However, other models have considered this effect, particularly for increased denaturation at pH levels over 5.0 \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}. Stomach acidity may approach this level after ingestion of a meal \\autocite{noauthor_regulation_2006}.\n\nOur model further assumed that pepsin was already active in the stomach upon food ingestion, ignoring preliminary activation of pepsinogen to pepsin by hydrochloric acid within the stomach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Stomach Emptying Regulator}\nAfter nutrients in the stomach have undergone enzymatic reactions and formed into acid chyme, they are emptied into the duodenum of the small intestine. This step is tightly controlled via the pyloric sphincter at a potential rate of 2--4 kilocalories per minute \\autocite{kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nOur simulation used a power exponential function reviewed by Kong et al. (2008). This model resembled other first-order exponential models discussed but included a power constant to account for an initial lag period in the emptying of solid foods. With this model, 50 percent of solids were expected to be emptied in approximately 94 minutes, and completely emptied in 2 or more hours \\autocite{siegel_biphasic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nWhile this model did not differentiate between distinct macronutrients, as chyme passes to the small intestine in real metabolism, a striation of chyme would be expected, with emulsified fats on top. Our simulation did not account for such separation and instead assumed that all nutrients were distributed and emptied in equal proportion.\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine}\n\\subsubsection{Pancreatic Amylase}\nThe model for pancreatic amylase matched the details of \\autoref{sec:amylase}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Trypsin}\nTo simplify, trypsin was the only enzyme considered in converting polypeptides within the small intestine into small peptide chains. However, a more robust model should also consider the presence of chymotrypsin and carboxypeptidase in this step.\n\nOur model based the catabolic rate of polypeptides on findings by Schwert and Eisenberg (1949) of the action of trypsin on benzoyl-L-argininamide. While the authors noted that the reaction theoretically requires higher-order modeling due to varying levels of competitive inhibition depending on substrate concentration, experimental results followed a zero-order (linear) hydrolysis rate of $1.1 \\times 10^{-3}$ millimoles per minute of polypeptides \\autocite{schwert_kinetics_1949}. This same zero-order model was selected for simulation.\n\n\\subsubsection{Lipase}\nLipases which catalyze the conversion of triglycerides to di- and mono-glycerides are present in the mouth, stomach, small intestine, and intestinal lining. Because most lipolysis occurs within the small intestine after emulsification via bile salts (not available to the stomach or mouth), only lipase within the small intestine (pancreatic lipase) was considered.\n\nThe selected model of pancreatic lipase presented distinct first-order reactions for the hydrolysis of triolein to 1,2-diolein, and then 1,2-diolein to 2-monolein. Each reaction was dependent on the initial concentration of triolein and rate constants for both substeps. Diglycerides were catabolized nearly three times faster than triglycerides \\autocite{lykidis_kinetics_1995}.\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine Lining}\n\\subsubsection{Maltose Hydrolysis}\nMaltose resulting from amylolysis earlier in the organ pipeline was hydrolyzed to glucose. This reaction is catalyzed by maltase. A Michaelis-Menten equation model was selected, whose numerical parameters were derived from experiments with rat small jejunums \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_2021}. \n\nTo apply this model to humans, a typical human small intestine length \\autocite{fish_physiology_2025} and fed intestinal fluid volume \\autocite{schiller_intestinal_2005} replaced the values used for rats.\n\n\\subsubsection{Peptidase}\nPeptidase catalyzes the hydrolysis reaction converting peptide chains into individual amino acids (all assumed to be glutamic acid). \n\nThe rate for this step is not sourced. For demonstration, it is assumed to be first-order, catabolizing 20 percent of existing small peptide chains during each time step.\n\n\\subsubsection{Mixed Micelle Formation}\nTo enter the enterocytes, monoglycerides and free fatty acids within the small intestinal lining first form emulsified mixed micelles with bile salts. \n\nKinetics for this process were not sourced. A naive model was implemented in which a single mole of monolein, two moles of oleic acid, and one mole of bile coalesce to form a mole of micelle. The lowest-quantity substrate was selected as the limiting reactant in each time step, and the reaction consumed 50 percent of the limiting reactant alongside corresponding proportions of both other substrates.\n\n\\subsubsection{Chylomicron Production}\nAfter passing into the enterocytes via mixed micelles, glycerides and fatty acids form into chylomicrons, which are water-soluble and hence may enter the bloodstream.\n\nEnterocytes were modeled as a distinct organ in the simulation to emphasize this step. For simplicity, 50 percent of micelles from the intestinal lining per time step were assumed to eventually produce chylomicrons, with all intermediate steps omitted.  \n\n\\subsection{Liver}\n\\subsubsection{Bile Salt Production}\nBile salt was modeled to be injected directly to the small intestine from the liver. However, in reality, bile would first be stored within the gallbladder and later delivered via the bile duct. Our modeled liver produced and transported 1 mmol of bile salt per time step.\n\n\\subsection{Pancreas}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Production}\n\\label{sec:insulin-production}\nInsulin production by the pancreas, and secretion to the bloodstream, relied on a model with both plasma glucose and plasma amino acids as regulators. To determine the needed level of insulin in a time step, the model assigned linear coefficients to current levels of glucose, amino acids, and insulin. Those coefficients were applied to the differences between current levels and assumed base levels, such that a higher change from base levels corresponded to a higher change in insulin \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}.\n\nWe adopted this model by communicating calculated values of glucose and glutamic acid within the simulated bloodstream to the pancreas node, which were used as parameters in the provided linear equation.\n\n\\subsection{Bloodstream}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Degradation}\nThe same model used in \\autoref{sec:insulin-production} accounted for insulin degradation within the blood via first-order feedback. Assuming a steady-state average insulin level $I_b$ of approximately 7.5 $\\mu$U/mL and a rate coefficient $k_I$ of 0.14, insulin in the next time step was denatured by \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_2022}:\n\n\\[Insulin(t) -\u003d k_I * (Insulin(t - 1) - I_b)\\]\n\nIn this manner, insulin was expected to naturally degrade to its base level in steady state. The specific kinetics of the insulin-degrading enzyme, which contribute to an expected insulin half-life of 4--6 minutes \\autocite{noauthor_chapter_2009}, were not included in this approach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glucose Uptake Regulator}\nInsulin levels in the blood affect the rate at which glucose is absorbed into cells. Passage of glucose from the blood across the cell membrane was implemented as a one-way edge between the blood and cytosol nodes. Glucose uptake regulation was implemented as a rate limiter for this edge.\n\nEstimated parameters were selected from a Michaelis-Menten equation of glucose uptake, fit to glucose and insulin readings of human adults. This equation modeled the maximum glucose uptake per unit time as a Hill function \\autocite{goutelle_hill_2008} of insulin concentration at the active site \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_2016}. \n\n\\subsection{Within Cells}\nDistinct areas of cells, including the cytosol, mitochondrial matrix, and inner mitochondrial membrane, were modeled as though the volume of participating cells of the body were combined into a single cell, with equal distribution of nutrients from the blood to all areas. This volume was visualized as single, pseudo-organ nodes for each area. Types of cells were not differentiated, and a total count of $3.00 \\times 10^{13}$ cells participating in glycolysis and energy generation was assumed \\autocite{hatton_human_2023}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glycolysis - Cytosol}\nGlucose which enters the cell cytosol undergoes glycolysis with the final output of pyruvate. The simulation assumed only aerobic conditions and, hence, that zero pyruvate was ever later reduced to lactic acid. \n\nFurther, glycolysis and other catabolic processes within the cells treated adenosine triphosphate (ATP) levels as effectively unlimited. Their availability was not considered for substrate inputs nor outputs (excluding the ETC).\n\nThe selected model considered seven different intermediate substrates, in order of appearance in glycolysis: glucose, glucose-6-phosphate (G6P), fructose-6-phosphate (F6P), fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (F16BP), 3-phosphoglycerate (3PG), phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP), and pyruvate. The rate of change of each was modeled with Michaelis-Menten and Hill kinetics as a function of other intermediates \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}.\n\n\\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} shows highly simplified relationships between each of these substrates. While the table omits feedback effects on particular glycolytic enzymes, expected examples of feed-forward and backward regulation are present. For example, F16BP increases the later production of PEP from 3PG by allosterically activating pyruvate kinase \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}. As a backwards-feedback example, G6P\u0027s inhibition of hexokinase is encoded in \\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} as \"self-regulation.\"\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Glycolysis ODE Metabolite Dependencies}\n\\label{tab:glycolysis-dependencies}\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.3} \n\\begin{NiceTabular}{llc}[\n    code-before \u003d {\n        \\rowcolors{2}{gray!10}{white}[cols\u003d{2-3}]\n    }\n]\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metabolite} \u0026 \\textbf{Dependency} \u0026 \\textbf{Effect} \\\\\n\\midrule\nGLC (Intracellular Glucose) \u0026 GLC \u0026 $\\pm$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{3-1}{G6P (Glucose-6-Phosphate)} \n    \u0026 GLC \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{2-1}{F6P (Fructose-6-Phosphate)} \n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{2-1}{F16BP (Fructose-1,6-Bisphosphate)} \n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{3-1}{3PG (3-Phosphoglycerate)} \n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{3-1}{PEP (Phosphoenolpyruvate)} \n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 $-$  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{2-1}{Pyruvate} \n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{NiceTabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nOur simulation lacked key glycolysis regulation steps due to the omission of non-substrate regulators. In particular, no enzymes were regulated by the presence of energy-yielding molecules such as ATP, adenosine diphosphate (ADP), and adenosine monophosphate (AMP).\n\n\\subsubsection{Fatty Acid Oxidation -- Mitochondrial Matrix}\nThe reactions below within the mitochondrial matrix are implemented naively in that inputs from previous steps were assumed to be converted directly into energy-yielding molecules. However, net quantities of energy-yielding molecules output were expected to be realistic. To retain a pool of cycle inputs for visualization, only 75 percent were processed in each time step for fatty acid oxidation, conversion of amino acids to alanine, and the TCA cycle.\n\n1 mole of Fatty acyl-CoA produced a mole of acetyl-CoA based on the length of the fatty acid carbon chain:\n\\[Acetyl-CoA \u003d N_C / 2 - 1\\]\nin addition to 1 mole NADH and 1 mole FADH$_2$ \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. As oleic acid has a carbon length of 18 \\autocite{pubchem_oleic_nodate}, each step produced 8 moles of Acetyl-CoA per mole of oleic acid.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amino Acid Catabolism - Mitochondrial Matrix}\nGlutamic acid and pyruvate react to form $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate and alanine when catalyzed by alanine aminotransferase \\autocite{lenartowicz_significance_1988}. $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate can then enter the TCA cycle (\\autoref{sec:tca}). Our model assumed that glutamic acid would always be the limiting substrate of this reaction, such that, in each time step, 75 percent of contained glutamic acid was consumed along with an equal number of moles of pyruvate.\n\n\\subsubsection{TCA Cycle - Mitochondrial Matrix}\n\\label{sec:tca}\nEach mole of acetyl-CoA and pyruvate produced 3 moles NADH, 1 mole FADH$_2$, and 1 mole guanosine triphosphate (GTP). $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate produced 2 moles NADH, 1 mole FADH$_2$, and 1 mole GTP \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. The mitochondrial matrix included two processes with the same input substrate—both amino acid catabolism and the TCA cycle required pyruvate. Distribution of pyruvate to these two processes was implemented via a simple, ordered list: first, all available glutamate was converted; then, all remaining pyruvate was passed to the numerical solver for the TCA.\n\n\\subsubsection{ETC - Mitochondrial Inner Membrane}\nThe ETC model assumed that 2 moles ATP were produced per mole NADH and 1.5 moles per mole FADH$_2$. The maximum production rate was 100 ATP molecules per minute per ATP-synthase \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. Total production considered the total number of active synthases as a product of synthases per mitochondria, mitochondria per cell, and total cells (\\nameref{sec:physical-constants}).\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\subsection{Strengths}\nThis simulation presented a unified set of researched reaction kinetics. While such kinetics of major metabolic steps are accessible in the literature, relatively little has been published combining many together to demonstrate larger-scale effects in the body.\n\nThis work closely coupled demonstrative code with an intuitive organization of metabolism and scientific sources. Based on a limited search, such design and prioritization of code accessibility appear to be novel in biochemical kinetics research.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\nAt the time of writing, this simulation is a prototype intended to demonstrate knowledge at an introductory graduate level. It included steps which are known to be based on incorrect assumptions and omitted important steps, particularly in regulation. As the software had not been part of a rigorous review, the code likely contained further defects.\n\nModeling for this simulation was not based on a thorough literature review. High-level model flow was taken from course lecture content \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}, and more specific sources for individual reactions were selected on their strengths as easily accessible and relevant parameterized models. Projected accuracy within the overall metabolic pipeline was not the highest priority.\n\n\\subsection{Future work}\nThe possible improvements noted above are strong candidates to improve the robustness of the presented simulation. To remove overly strong assumptions, a literature review should be conducted to better select models for each interaction. More recently researched models should be added, including realistic models of the transfer of metabolites between organs and organelles (not considered in the current simulation).\n\nThe code should be reviewed and improved for maintainability by multiple investigators. It can then be considered a tool to continue mapping intuitive physical body structure to functioning code which demonstrates the current state of the literature. Such a tool will be useful to unify metabolism research and identify gaps.\n\nNo anabolic processes have yet been included, such as the transportation of glycerol from triglyceride catabolism to the liver for either gluconeogenesis or fatty acid reconstruction. Addition of these processes would augment the demonstrative capability of this simulation as a learning tool. Further, the developed codebase is equipped to support such new transports and processes with minimal time investment.\n\n\n\\section{Appendix I: Physical Constants} \n\\label{sec:physical-constants}\n\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.4}\n\\small\n\\newcolumntype{Y}{\u003e{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X}\n\n\\begin{xltabular}{\\textwidth}{Y l l Y l}\n    \\caption{Physical Constants Used in Metabolism Simulation} \\label{tab:physical-constants} \\\\\n    \\toprule\n    \\textbf{Constant} \u0026 \\textbf{Value} \u0026 \\textbf{Units} \u0026 \\textbf{Metabolic Process(es)} \u0026 \\textbf{Source} \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    \\endfirsthead\n\n    \\multicolumn{5}{c}{\\textit{Table \\thetable{} continued from previous page}} \\\\\n    \\toprule\n