        "text": "table}\n\n\\subsection{Mouth}\nThe mouth was assumed to fully empty its contents into the stomach within a"
      }, {
        "text": " single, one-minute time step. The presence of the esophagus between the mouth and stomach was omitted.\n\n\\subsubsection"
      }, {
        "text": "{Amylase}\n\\label{sec:amylase}\nFor simplicity, starch is treated as 1"
      }, {
        "text": "00 percent amylose, which is converted to maltose by amylolysis. However, real starch metabolism would also include"
      }, {
        "text": " a pathway to catabolize contained amylopectin \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Real"
      }, {
        "text": " amylolysis would also produce maltotriose and $\\alpha$-limit dextrins \\autocite{roberts_mechanism"
      }, {
        "text": "_1960, butterworth_enzyme_2022}. \n\nThe mouth contains salivary amylase"
      }, {
        "text": ", and other types of amylase are included in organs below. All amylase reactions were modeled after the same kinetics for"
      }, {
        "text": " pancreatic amylase, a first-order exponential with a rate constant of 0.03 (or starch half-"
      }, {
        "text": "life of 23 minutes) \\autocite{butterworth_analysis_2012}. Because a"
      }, {
        "text": " model of amylase action specifically on peanuts was not available, the results provided by Butterworth et al. (201"
      }, {
        "text": "2) for beans were selected. Peanuts and beans are in the same legume family and share similarities in macronutrients \\"
      }, {
        "text": "autocite{toomer_nutritional_2018}.\n\n\\subsection{Stomach}\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "subsubsection{Gastric Amylase}\nThe same model presented in \\autoref{sec:amylase} acted within"
      }, {
        "text": " the stomach on remaining starch passed from the mouth.\n\n\\subsubsection{Pepsin}\nAs no protein catabolism was considered within"
      }, {
        "text": " the mouth, all proteins (arachins) were passed directly to the stomach. Pepsin converted arachin into arachin"
      }, {
        "text": " polypeptides. As a proxy for this reaction, a model fitting the hydrolysis of milk casein by pepsin was used, with"
      }, {
        "text": " a first-order exponential rate constant of 0.08 (protein half-life of approximately 9 minutes)"
      }, {
        "text": " \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}.\n\nEffects on the reaction from pepsin denaturation"
      }, {
        "text": " were not considered. However, other models have considered this effect, particularly for increased denaturation at pH levels over 5."
      }, {
        "text": "0 \\autocite{yang_kinetics_2022}. Stomach acidity may approach this level after ingestion"
      }, {
        "text": " of a meal \\autocite{noauthor_regulation_2006}.\n\nOur model further assumed that"
      }, {
        "text": " pepsin was already active in the stomach upon food ingestion, ignoring preliminary activation of pepsinogen to pepsin by hydrochloric"
      }, {
        "text": " acid within the stomach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Stomach Emptying Regulator}\nAfter nutrients in the stomach have undergone enzymatic reactions and"
      }, {
        "text": " formed into acid chyme, they are emptied into the duodenum of the small intestine. This step is tightly controlled via the"
      }, {
        "text": " pyloric sphincter at a potential rate of 2--4 kilocalories per minute \\autocite{kong_disintegration_"
      }, {
        "text": "2008}.\n\nOur simulation used a power exponential function reviewed by Kong et al. (2008"
      }, {
        "text": "). This model resembled other first-order exponential models discussed but included a power constant to account for an initial lag period in"
      }, {
        "text": " the emptying of solid foods. With this model, 50 percent of solids were expected to be emptied in approximately "
      }, {
        "text": "94 minutes, and completely emptied in 2 or more hours \\autocite{siegel_biphas"
      }, {
        "text": "ic_1988, kong_disintegration_2008}.\n\nWhile this model did not differentiate"
      }, {
        "text": " between distinct macronutrients, as chyme passes to the small intestine in real metabolism, a striation of chyme would be expected"
      }, {
        "text": ", with emulsified fats on top. Our simulation did not account for such separation and instead assumed that all nutrients were distributed and emptied"
      }, {
        "text": " in equal proportion.\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine}\n\\subsubsection{Pancreatic Amylase}\nThe"
      }, {
        "text": " model for pancreatic amylase matched the details of \\autoref{sec:amylase}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Trypsin}"
      }, {
        "text": "\nTo simplify, trypsin was the only enzyme considered in converting polypeptides within the small intestine into small peptide chains. However,"
      }, {
        "text": " a more robust model should also consider the presence of chymotrypsin and carboxypeptidase in this step.\n\nOur model based"
      }, {
        "text": " the catabolic rate of polypeptides on findings by Schwert and Eisenberg (1949) of the action of"
      }, {
        "text": " trypsin on benzoyl-L-argininamide. While the authors noted that the reaction theoretically requires higher-order modeling"
      }, {
        "text": " due to varying levels of competitive inhibition depending on substrate concentration, experimental results followed a zero-order (linear) hydrolysis rate"
      }, {
        "text": " of $1.1 \\times 10^{-3}$ millimoles per minute of polypeptides \\autocite{sch"
      }, {
        "text": "wert_kinetics_1949}. This same zero-order model was selected for simulation.\n\n\\subsubsection"
      }, {
        "text": "{Lipase}\nLipases which catalyze the conversion of triglycerides to di- and mono-glycerides are present in the mouth"
      }, {
        "text": ", stomach, small intestine, and intestinal lining. Because most lipolysis occurs within the small intestine after emulsification via bile"
      }, {
        "text": " salts (not available to the stomach or mouth), only lipase within the small intestine (pancreatic lipase) was considered."
      }, {
        "text": "\n\nThe selected model of pancreatic lipase presented distinct first-order reactions for the hydrolysis of triolein to 1,2-dio"
      }, {
        "text": "lein, and then 1,2-diolein to 2-monolein. Each reaction was dependent on the initial concentration"
      }, {
        "text": " of triolein and rate constants for both substeps. Diglycerides were catabolized nearly three times faster than triglycerides \\"
      }, {
        "text": "autocite{lykidis_kinetics_1995}.\n\n\\subsection{Small Intestine Lining}\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "subsubsection{Maltose Hydrolysis}\nMaltose resulting from amylolysis earlier in the organ pipeline was hydrolyzed to"
      }, {
        "text": " glucose. This reaction is catalyzed by maltase. A Michaelis-Menten equation model was selected, whose numerical parameters"
      }, {
        "text": " were derived from experiments with rat small jejunums \\autocite{gromova_mechanisms_20"
      }, {
        "text": "21}. \n\nTo apply this model to humans, a typical human small intestine length \\autocite{fish"
      }, {
        "text": "_physiology_2025} and fed intestinal fluid volume \\autocite{schiller_intestinal_2"
      }, {
        "text": "005} replaced the values used for rats.\n\n\\subsubsection{Peptidase}\nPeptidase catalyzes the hydrolysis reaction converting"
      }, {
        "text": " peptide chains into individual amino acids (all assumed to be glutamic acid). \n\nThe rate for this step is not"
      }, {
        "text": " sourced. For demonstration, it is assumed to be first-order, catabolizing 20 percent of existing small"
      }, {
        "text": " peptide chains during each time step.\n\n\\subsubsection{Mixed Micelle Formation}\nTo enter the enterocytes, mon"
      }, {
        "text": "oglycerides and free fatty acids within the small intestinal lining first form emulsified mixed micelles with bile salts. \n\n"
      }, {
        "text": "Kinetics for this process were not sourced. A naive model was implemented in which a single mole of monolein,"
      }, {
        "text": " two moles of oleic acid, and one mole of bile coalesce to form a mole of micelle. The lowest-"
      }, {
        "text": "quantity substrate was selected as the limiting reactant in each time step, and the reaction consumed 50 percent of the limiting"
      }, {
        "text": " reactant alongside corresponding proportions of both other substrates.\n\n\\subsubsection{Chylomicron Production}\nAfter passing into the enterocytes via"
      }, {
        "text": " mixed micelles, glycerides and fatty acids form into chylomicrons, which are water-soluble and hence may enter the bloodstream"
      }, {
        "text": ".\n\nEnterocytes were modeled as a distinct organ in the simulation to emphasize this step. For simplicity, 50 percent of micelles"
      }, {
        "text": " from the intestinal lining per time step were assumed to eventually produce chylomicrons, with all intermediate steps omitted.  "
      }, {
        "text": "\n\n\\subsection{Liver}\n\\subsubsection{Bile Salt Production}\nBile salt was modeled to be injected directly"
      }, {
        "text": " to the small intestine from the liver. However, in reality, bile would first be stored within the gallbladder and later delivered via"
      }, {
        "text": " the bile duct. Our modeled liver produced and transported 1 mmol of bile salt per time step.\n\n\\subsection{Pan"
      }, {
        "text": "creas}\n\\subsubsection{Insulin Production}\n\\label{sec:insulin-production}\nInsulin production"
      }, {
        "text": " by the pancreas, and secretion to the bloodstream, relied on a model with both plasma glucose and plasma amino acids as regulators"
      }, {
        "text": ". To determine the needed level of insulin in a time step, the model assigned linear coefficients to current levels of glucose,"
      }, {
        "text": " amino acids, and insulin. Those coefficients were applied to the differences between current levels and assumed base levels, such that a"
      }, {
        "text": " higher change from base levels corresponded to a higher change in insulin \\autocite{morettini_mathematical_"
      }, {
        "text": "2022}.\n\nWe adopted this model by communicating calculated values of glucose and glutamic acid within the simulated bloodstream"
      }, {
        "text": " to the pancreas node, which were used as parameters in the provided linear equation.\n\n\\subsection{Bloodstream}\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\subsubsection{Insulin Degradation}\nThe same model used in \\autoref{sec:insulin-production} accounted"
      }, {
        "text": " for insulin degradation within the blood via first-order feedback. Assuming a steady-state average insulin level $I_b"
      }, {
        "text": "$ of approximately 7.5 $\\mu$U/mL and a rate coefficient $k_I$ of 0."
      }, {
        "text": "14, insulin in the next time step was denatured by \\autocite{morettini_mathematical"
      }, {
        "text": "_2022}:\n\n\\[Insulin(t) -\u003d k_I * (Insulin(t -"
      }, {
        "text": " 1) - I_b)\\]\n\nIn this manner, insulin was expected to naturally degrade to its base level"
      }, {
        "text": " in steady state. The specific kinetics of the insulin-degrading enzyme, which contribute to an expected insulin half-life"
      }, {
        "text": " of 4--6 minutes \\autocite{noauthor_chapter_2009}, were not included"
      }, {
        "text": " in this approach.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glucose Uptake Regulator}\nInsulin levels in the blood affect the rate at"
      }, {
        "text": " which glucose is absorbed into cells. Passage of glucose from the blood across the cell membrane was implemented as a one-way"
      }, {
        "text": " edge between the blood and cytosol nodes. Glucose uptake regulation was implemented as a rate limiter for this edge.\n\nEstimated parameters"
      }, {
        "text": " were selected from a Michaelis-Menten equation of glucose uptake, fit to glucose and insulin readings of human adults."
      }, {
        "text": " This equation modeled the maximum glucose uptake per unit time as a Hill function \\autocite{goutelle_hill"
      }, {
        "text": "_2008} of insulin concentration at the active site \\autocite{bizzotto_glucose_"
      }, {
        "text": "2016}. \n\n\\subsection{Within Cells}\nDistinct areas of cells, including the cytosol, mitochondrial"
      }, {
        "text": " matrix, and inner mitochondrial membrane, were modeled as though the volume of participating cells of the body were combined into a single"
      }, {
        "text": " cell, with equal distribution of nutrients from the blood to all areas. This volume was visualized as single, pseudo-organ"
      }, {
        "text": " nodes for each area. Types of cells were not differentiated, and a total count of $3.00 \\times 1"
      }, {
        "text": "0^{13}$ cells participating in glycolysis and energy generation was assumed \\autocite{hatton_human_"
      }, {
        "text": "2023}.\n\n\\subsubsection{Glycolysis - Cytosol}\nGlucose which enters the cell cytosol"
      }, {
        "text": " undergoes glycolysis with the final output of pyruvate. The simulation assumed only aerobic conditions and, hence, that zero pyruvate was ever"
      }, {
        "text": " later reduced to lactic acid. \n\nFurther, glycolysis and other catabolic processes within the cells treated adenosine triphosphate (ATP"
      }, {
        "text": ") levels as effectively unlimited. Their availability was not considered for substrate inputs nor outputs (excluding the ETC).\n\nThe selected model"
      }, {
        "text": " considered seven different intermediate substrates, in order of appearance in glycolysis: glucose, glucose-6-phosphate (G6P), fructose-"
      }, {
        "text": "6-phosphate (F6P), fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (F16BP), 3-phosph"
      }, {
        "text": "oglycerate (3PG), phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP), and pyruvate. The rate of change of each was"
      }, {
        "text": " modeled with Michaelis-Menten and Hill kinetics as a function of other intermediates \\autocite{rehberg_"
      }, {
        "text": "glycolysis_2014}.\n\n\\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} shows highly simplified relationships between each"
      }, {
        "text": " of these substrates. While the table omits feedback effects on particular glycolytic enzymes, expected examples of feed-forward and"
      }, {
        "text": " backward regulation are present. For example, F16BP increases the later production of PEP from 3PG by allosterically activating"
      }, {
        "text": " pyruvate kinase \\autocite{rehberg_glycolysis_2014}. As a backwards-feedback example,"
      }, {
        "text": " G6P\u0027s inhibition of hexokinase is encoded in \\autoref{tab:glycolysis-dependencies} as"
      }, {
        "text": " \"self-regulation.\"\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Glycolysis ODE"
      }, {
        "text": " Metabolite Dependencies}\n\\label{tab:glycolysis-dependencies}\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1"
      }, {
        "text": ".3} \n\\begin{NiceTabular}{llc}[\n    code-before \u003d {\n        "
      }, {
        "text": "\\rowcolors{2}{gray!10}{white}[cols\u003d{2-3}]\n    }\n]"
      }, {
        "text": "\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metabolite} \u0026 \\textbf{Dependency} \u0026 \\textbf{Effect} \\\\"
      }, {
        "text": "\n\\midrule\nGLC (Intracellular Glucose) \u0026 GLC \u0026 $\\pm$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\midrule\n\\Block{3-1}{G6P (Glucose-6-Phosphate)} \n    "
      }, {
        "text": "\u0026 GLC \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 $-$ (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F6"
      }, {
        "text": "P \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{2-1}{F6P (Fructose-6"
      }, {
        "text": "-Phosphate)} \n    \u0026 G6P \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F6P \u0026 $-$ (self"
