      }, {
        "text": "oglycerate)} \n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 $-$ ("
      }, {
        "text": "self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 +  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{3-1}{"
      }, {
        "text": "PEP (Phosphoenolpyruvate)} \n    \u0026 3PG \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 $-$"
      }, {
        "text": " (self-limiting) \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 $-$  \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\Block{"
      }, {
        "text": "2-1}{Pyruvate} \n    \u0026 PEP \u0026 + \\\\\n    \u0026 F16BP \u0026 +"
      }, {
        "text": "  \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{NiceTabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nOur simulation lacked key glycolysis"
      }, {
        "text": " regulation steps due to the omission of non-substrate regulators. In particular, no enzymes were regulated by the presence of energy"
      }, {
        "text": "-yielding molecules such as ATP, adenosine diphosphate (ADP), and adenosine monophosphate (AMP).\n\n\\subsubsection{Fat"
      }, {
        "text": "ty Acid Oxidation -- Mitochondrial Matrix}\nThe reactions below within the mitochondrial matrix are implemented naively in that inputs from previous steps were"
      }, {
        "text": " assumed to be converted directly into energy-yielding molecules. However, net quantities of energy-yielding molecules output were expected to"
      }, {
        "text": " be realistic. To retain a pool of cycle inputs for visualization, only 75 percent were processed in each time step"
      }, {
        "text": " for fatty acid oxidation, conversion of amino acids to alanine, and the TCA cycle.\n\n1 mole of Fatty acyl-"
      }, {
        "text": "CoA produced a mole of acetyl-CoA based on the length of the fatty acid carbon chain:\n\\[Acetyl-CoA \u003d N_"
      }, {
        "text": "C / 2 - 1\\]\nin addition to 1 mole NADH and 1 mole FADH$_2"
      }, {
        "text": "$ \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. As oleic acid has a carbon length of "
      }, {
        "text": "18 \\autocite{pubchem_oleic_nodate}, each step produced 8 moles of Acetyl-CoA per"
      }, {
        "text": " mole of oleic acid.\n\n\\subsubsection{Amino Acid Catabolism - Mitochondrial Matrix}\nGlutamic acid and"
      }, {
        "text": " pyruvate react to form $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate and alanine when catalyzed by alanine aminotransferase \\autocite{lenart"
      }, {
        "text": "owicz_significance_1988}. $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate can then enter the TCA cycle (\\"
      }, {
        "text": "autoref{sec:tca}). Our model assumed that glutamic acid would always be the limiting substrate of this reaction,"
      }, {
        "text": " such that, in each time step, 75 percent of contained glutamic acid was consumed along with an equal number"
      }, {
        "text": " of moles of pyruvate.\n\n\\subsubsection{TCA Cycle - Mitochondrial Matrix}\n\\label{sec:tca"
      }, {
        "text": "}\nEach mole of acetyl-CoA and pyruvate produced 3 moles NADH, 1 mole FADH$_2$, and 1"
      }, {
        "text": " mole guanosine triphosphate (GTP). $\\alpha$-ketoglutarate produced 2 moles NADH, 1 mole F"
      }, {
        "text": "ADH$_2$, and 1 mole GTP \\autocite{brunton2025lecture}. The mitochondrial matrix included two"
      }, {
        "text": " processes with the same input substrate—both amino acid catabolism and the TCA cycle required pyruvate. Distribution of pyruvate to these"
      }, {
        "text": " two processes was implemented via a simple, ordered list: first, all available glutamate was converted; then, all remaining pyruvate was passed"
      }, {
        "text": " to the numerical solver for the TCA.\n\n\\subsubsection{ETC - Mitochondrial Inner Membrane}\nThe ETC model assumed that"
      }, {
        "text": " 2 moles ATP were produced per mole NADH and 1.5 moles per mole FADH$_2$. The maximum"
      }, {
        "text": " production rate was 100 ATP molecules per minute per ATP-synthase \\autocite{brunton2"
      }, {
        "text": "025lecture}. Total production considered the total number of active synthases as a product of synthases per mitochondria, mitochondria"
      }, {
        "text": " per cell, and total cells (\\nameref{sec:physical-constants}).\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\"
      }, {
        "text": "subsection{Strengths}\nThis simulation presented a unified set of researched reaction kinetics. While such kinetics of major metabolic steps"
      }, {
        "text": " are accessible in the literature, relatively little has been published combining many together to demonstrate larger-scale effects in the body."
      }, {
        "text": "\n\nThis work closely coupled demonstrative code with an intuitive organization of metabolism and scientific sources. Based on a limited search,"
      }, {
        "text": " such design and prioritization of code accessibility appear to be novel in biochemical kinetics research.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\nAt"
      }, {
        "text": " the time of writing, this simulation is a prototype intended to demonstrate knowledge at an introductory graduate level. It included steps which"
      }, {
        "text": " are known to be based on incorrect assumptions and omitted important steps, particularly in regulation. As the software had not been part"
      }, {
        "text": " of a rigorous review, the code likely contained further defects.\n\nModeling for this simulation was not based on a thorough literature"
      }, {
        "text": " review. High-level model flow was taken from course lecture content \\autocite{brunton2025"
      }, {
        "text": "lecture}, and more specific sources for individual reactions were selected on their strengths as easily accessible and relevant parameterized models. Projected accuracy within the"
      }, {
        "text": " overall metabolic pipeline was not the highest priority.\n\n\\subsection{Future work}\nThe possible improvements noted above are strong candidates to improve"
      }, {
        "text": " the robustness of the presented simulation. To remove overly strong assumptions, a literature review should be conducted to better select models for"
      }, {
        "text": " each interaction. More recently researched models should be added, including realistic models of the transfer of metabolites between organs and organelles ("
      }, {
        "text": "not considered in the current simulation).\n\nThe code should be reviewed and improved for maintainability by multiple investigators. It can then be considered"
      }, {
        "text": " a tool to continue mapping intuitive physical body structure to functioning code which demonstrates the current state of the literature. Such a tool will be useful"
      }, {
        "text": " to unify metabolism research and identify gaps.\n\nNo anabolic processes have yet been included, such as the transportation of glycerol from triglyceride catabolism"
      }, {
        "text": " to the liver for either gluconeogenesis or fatty acid reconstruction. Addition of these processes would augment the demonstrative capability of this"
      }, {
        "text": " simulation as a learning tool. Further, the developed codebase is equipped to support such new transports and processes with minimal time investment.\n\n\n"
      }, {
        "text": "\\section{Appendix I: Physical Constants} \n\\label{sec:physical-constants}\n\n\\renewcommand{\\"
      }, {
        "text": "arraystretch}{1.4}\n\\small\n\\newcolumntype{Y}{\u003e{\\ragged"
      }, {
        "text": "right\\arraybackslash}X}\n\n\\begin{xltabular}{\\textwidth}{Y l l Y l}"
      }, {
        "text": "\n    \\caption{Physical Constants Used in Metabolism Simulation} \\label{tab:physical-constants} \\\\\n    "
      }, {
        "text": "\\toprule\n    \\textbf{Constant} \u0026 \\textbf{Value} \u0026 \\textbf{Units} \u0026 \\textbf"
      }, {
